course: dictionary
lesson: 9893
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so context clues help you choose the right one. «Hamish's dad is a fisherman in Inverness: one month it's takeaway twice a week, the next it's beans on toast.» Beans on toast is a simple meal, so takeaway here means a meal bought already cooked and eaten at home.
explain: «Having the kitchen redone means builders take the old one out and put a new one in, so for a while there is nowhere to cook and families eat takeaway.» With nowhere to cook, the clue is clear: takeaway is food someone else has cooked.
explain: In a meeting, «What's the key takeaway?» asks for the main point to remember. In maths, "take away" is two words and means subtract. Always check the sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: A takeaway (noun) is a meal you buy cooked from a shop or restaurant and eat at home.
fact: The shop itself can also be called a takeaway. In the USA and Canada, the word is takeout.
fact: Informally, a takeaway is the main idea or lesson you remember from a talk or meeting.
fact: In maths, "take away" (two words) means subtract: 8 take away 3 is 5.
fact: Takeaway can also be an adjective, as in takeaway food.
exercise: «On Friday nights many families get a takeaway: fish and chips, pizza, curry or Chinese.» What is a takeaway in this sentence? | A kitchen cupboard | A bag for carrying shopping home | *A meal bought cooked and eaten at home | A lesson learned from a talk
exercise: In a meeting, the teacher asks, "What's the key takeaway?" What does she mean? | The room where the meeting is held | A snack to eat during the meeting | A question with no answer | *The main point to remember
exercise: In maths, "8 take away 3 is 5." What does take away mean? | *Subtract | Add | Multiply | Divide
test: «Knowing a few simple meals saves money and keeps you healthy; adults who cannot cook spend much more on takeaways and ready meals.» What does takeaways mean in this sentence? | Fresh vegetables from a garden | Plates, cups and bowls | *Meals bought cooked from a shop | Lessons learned from a class
test: In the phrase «takeaway food», what kind of word is takeaway? | A verb | A noun | *An adjective | A question word
exam: «The Chinese takeaway on the high street is open until eleven.» Here takeaway means | A subtraction sum | The main lesson from a talk | A bag for carrying food home | *The shop or restaurant that sells cooked meals to eat at home
